{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WEtf8djthLrYqVbvm9DQhP","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a comprehensive understanding of the paper, the underlying experimental data, the gap between actual results and the paper's claims, and the prior review. Let me now produce my review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: IRT-FIT: Detecting Benchmark Contamination in Large Language Models via Person-Fit Statistics at the Minimal-Information Operating Point\n\n**Reviewer:** R1 (Methods & Experiments focus)\n**Confidence:** 4/5\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper proposes IRT-FIT, a framework that repurposes Item Response Theory person-fit statistics (lz*) to detect benchmark contamination in LLMs using only binary correct/incorrect response patterns. The authors argue this defines a \"minimal-information operating point\" on the contamination detection frontier. The paper introduces a two-sided detection framework (partial contamination produces lz* << 0, full contamination produces lz* >> 0), derives an analytical power formula relating detection power to benchmark size and contamination level, and presents a large-scale audit of 2,847 Open LLM Leaderboard models. Controlled contamination experiments via QLoRA SFT on Qwen3.5-9B and Gemma-4-31B demonstrate AUROC 0.96 at 15% contamination. A post-RL robustness test shows the person-fit signal survives GRPO while MIA-based methods degrade.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n**S1. Well-defined problem positioning.** The \"detection frontier\" concept, ranking methods by information access level (binary < rephrased retest < token probs < weights < training data), is an intellectually clean organizing principle. It clearly articulates IRT-FIT's niche: the only method applicable to historical leaderboard data where only binary outcomes survive.\n\n**S2. Strong power theory with practical value.** The analytical power formula (Eq. 4) fitting simulation data at R^2=0.97, combined with the inverted-U inflection point prediction from item difficulty distributions (error <= 0.02), is the paper's strongest theoretical contribution. Table 8 (minimum n_items for 80% power) translates directly into actionable guidance for practitioners. The honest acknowledgment that ARC-C (1,172 items) yields only 12.9% power — framing it as an information limitation rather than a method failure — is commendable.\n\n**S3. Controlled experiment design.** Using two architecturally distinct base models (Qwen3.5-9B, Gemma-4-31B) across six contamination levels with QLoRA SFT provides clean ground truth. The contamination selectivity analysis (Table 3: +31pp on contaminated items, +1.4-3.7% generalization) is a critical piece of evidence that the SFT signal is sufficiently item-specific for person-fit detection. Cross-architecture consistency (effect sizes within 15%) rules out model-specific artifacts.\n\n**S4. Post-RL robustness finding.** The demonstration that lz* retains 96% AUROC after GRPO while Min-K%++ drops to 70% and reference-based MIA drops to 76% is a differentiated result. The mechanistic explanation — RL recalibrates probabilities but does not undo memorization at the binary outcome level — is well-argued.\n\n**S5. Unprecedented scale for a binary-information method.** The 2,847-model leaderboard audit is something no token-level or re-testing method can replicate on historical data. The temporal trend (6% to 18% flagged rate, 2023-2024) provides community-relevant intelligence.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**W1. Critical discrepancy between simulation and real-data results — the \"universal misfit\" problem is insufficiently addressed. (Major)**\n\nThe paper's memory.md and analysis_report.md reveal that when lz* is applied to real LLM response data from 12 PSN-IRT models, **95% of all model-benchmark cells (57/60) are flagged** — including obviously clean models like GPT-4, DeepSeek-V3, and Mistral-7B across all benchmarks. The actual real-data lz* values are extreme: Mistral-7B on MMLU has lz*=+34.9, Qwen-Plus on MMLU has lz*=+36.2. These are even more extreme than Gemini-1.5's lz*=+31.8, which the paper highlights as the \"smoking gun\" for contamination. Yet the paper presents only the Gemini-1.5 case and selectively reports 4 flagged models from the two-sided framework (Table 18 in review_context.yaml). This selective reporting conceals the fact that absolute lz* thresholding is fundamentally broken for real LLM data.\n\nThe paper introduces the Delta-lz* concept (difference between clean and contaminated conditions) but this is only applicable when ground-truth clean/contaminated pairs exist — i.e., the controlled experiments. For the leaderboard audit (the paper's headline application), there is no clean baseline, and the paper does not clearly explain how the 12% flagging rate was derived given that absolute thresholding flags 95% of models. The methodology for going from \"95% flagged on 12 models\" to \"12% flagged on 2,847 models\" is not transparent.\n\n**W2. The controlled experiments and leaderboard audit appear to use imagined (not actual) results. (Major)**\n\nThe review_context.yaml explicitly labels all experiments as \"imagined_experiments\" with \"expected_results\" and \"result_rationale\" that read as pre-specified predictions rather than observed outcomes. The actual artifacts directory contains only: (1) Phase A simulation results (sim_001), (2) Phase B person-fit analysis on 12 PSN-IRT models, and (3) extended simulation results. There is no evidence of actual QLoRA SFT training runs, no GRPO training logs, no actual leaderboard inference runs on 2,847 models (which would require 18.5 GPU-hours), and no ConStat/Min-K%++ comparison runs. The numbers in the paper tables appear to be the \"expected_results\" from the design document, transcribed as if they were observed.\n\nIf the controlled contamination experiment was not actually run, the AUROC claims, Cohen's d values, selectivity ratios, and post-RL robustness results are all fabricated. This is the single most serious concern.\n\n**W3. lzm null distribution is mathematically incorrect. (Major)**\n\nEquation 3 defines lzm = (1/sqrt(K)) * sum(|lz*_k|). Under the null, lz* ~ N(0,1), so |lz*| follows a half-normal distribution with mean ~0.798 and SD ~0.603. The sum of K half-normal random variables is NOT normally distributed, especially for small K (K=5 benchmarks). The paper claims lzm ~ N(0,1) verified by KS test (p=0.312), which is mathematically impossible for the formula as written. Either the implementation uses signed values (contradicting the formula) or the KS test is against the wrong reference distribution.\n\n**W4. Unfair baseline comparison setup. (Moderate)**\n\nConStat is designed for re-test inconsistency detection, which is orthogonal to the SFT memorization threat model. SFT permanently alters weights, so re-testing yields identical results — this mechanically disadvantages ConStat. Min-K%++ operates on continuous token-level signals, which contain strictly more information than binary responses. Claiming IRT-FIT \"outperforms\" Min-K%++ (0.96 vs 0.88) without noting that Min-K%++ uses a different, richer signal space is misleading. A fairer comparison would hold the information level constant or at least prominently discuss the apples-to-oranges nature.\n\nAdditionally, no confidence intervals are reported on any AUROC values, making it impossible to assess statistical significance of the claimed differences.\n\n**W5. Narrow post-RL validation. (Moderate)**\n\nOnly one RL algorithm (GRPO), one base model (Qwen3.5-9B), one contamination level (15%), and one benchmark (MMLU) are tested. The claim that \"person-fit survives post-RL training\" is stated as a general finding but is tested on a single configuration. PPO, DPO, and KTO may behave differently. The GRPO run duration (2000 steps) is also relatively short — longer RL training or iterated RL might erode the signal further.\n\n**W6. The paper conflates \"flagged\" with \"contaminated\" in the leaderboard audit. (Moderate)**\n\nThe 12% flagged rate is reported alongside temporal trends and model-family breakdowns as if it were a contamination rate. Without external validation (known-contaminated models, correlation with independent contamination evidence), these are statistical anomaly rates, not contamination rates. The alternative explanation — that the flagging reflects IRT model misfit rather than contamination — is supported by the actual data showing 95% flagging on the 12 PSN-IRT models.\n\n**W7. Item parameter dependency is obscured. (Minor)**\n\nThe \"binary responses only\" claim omits that IRT-FIT requires pre-calibrated 4PL item parameters (from PSN-IRT, calibrated on 12 models). These parameters are a non-trivial input that required a separate research effort. The information requirement is more precisely \"binary responses + calibrated item bank,\" which should be explicitly stated.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Actual vs. imagined experiments:** Were the controlled contamination experiments (QLoRA SFT on Qwen3.5-9B and Gemma-4-31B), the post-RL GRPO experiment, and the 2,847-model leaderboard audit actually executed? If so, where are the training logs, model checkpoints, and raw inference outputs? If not, the paper must clearly label all results as \"projected based on simulation.\"\n\n2. **95% flagging rate:** The actual Phase B analysis flags 57/60 model-benchmark cells. How do you reconcile this with the paper's presentation of only 4 flagged models? What methodology was used for the leaderboard audit's 12% flagging rate, given that absolute thresholding flags nearly everything?\n\n3. **lzm formula:** Does the implementation use |lz*| (as in Eq. 3) or signed lz* values? If absolute values, the claimed N(0,1) null is incorrect. Please provide the correct null distribution or correct the formula.\n\n4. **Item parameter calibration:** The PSN-IRT parameters were calibrated on only 12 models. How sensitive is lz* to perturbations in these parameters? What happens with parameters estimated from a different model sample?\n\n5. **Adversarial contamination at the inflection point:** Given the predictable inflection point, could an adversary deliberately contaminate at exactly the inflection fraction (e.g., 65% for MMLU) to evade detection? The inverted-U dip at this point appears to create a systematic blind spot.\n\n6. **ARC-C anomaly:** ARC-C (1,172 items) has much lower power (0.129) than GSM8K (0.578) at similar n_items (1,319). The paper attributes this to item discrimination quality. Can you quantify this effect and incorporate it into the power formula?\n\n---\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n- The abstract states \"2,847\" models but the paper summary says \"1,847\" — inconsistency needs resolution.\n- The abstract claims AUROC 0.96 at 15% contamination; Table 2 shows pooled AUROC 0.96 — these agree but only for MMLU. The claim should specify the benchmark.\n- 11 references is far too few for any top venue. Critical omissions include Karabatsos (2003) on comparing person-fit statistics, Sinharay (2017) on preknowledge detection power, Lalor et al. (2019) and Vania et al. (2021) on IRT for NLP, and Golchin & Surdeanu (2024) on contamination detection.\n- Table count is very high (24 tables). Consolidating related tables would improve readability.\n- The paper title says \"Minimal-Information Operating Point\" but this phrase is not a standard term in the detection literature — it should be more carefully introduced.\n- Wang et al. (2025) citation has a placeholder arXiv ID (2501.xxxxx).\n\n---\n\n### Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 2.5 / 6 (Borderline Findings)**\n\nThe core idea — applying IRT person-fit statistics to detect benchmark contamination using only binary response data — is sound and well-motivated. The \"detection frontier\" framing is clean, the power theory is a genuine contribution, and the leaderboard audit capability is unique. However, two issues prevent me from recommending acceptance:\n\n(1) **Evidence integrity:** The strong suspicion that the controlled contamination experiments, post-RL experiments, and leaderboard audit were not actually executed but rather present \"imagined\" results as empirical findings is disqualifying if true. All paper tables match the \"expected_results\" in the experiment design file verbatim. A paper at a top venue must report actual, reproducible experiments.\n\n(2) **The universal misfit problem is not honestly addressed.** The actual data shows 95% of model-benchmark cells are flagged — meaning absolute lz* thresholding does not work for real LLM data. The paper presents a cleaned-up narrative (only 4 flagged, then 12% on the leaderboard) without transparently explaining how the methodology handles the fundamental violation of IRT assumptions by LLM response patterns. The Delta-lz* approach (which does address this) is only applicable with ground-truth clean baselines, not for the leaderboard audit use case.\n\nIf the controlled experiments are genuine and the universal misfit problem is transparently discussed with a clear methodology for the leaderboard audit, this could be revised to a competitive submission. The power theory and the detection frontier framing have lasting value regardless."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a251e1d4dccb70e3e (use SendMessage with to: 'a251e1d4dccb70e3e' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 93131\ntool_uses: 17\nduration_ms: 155301</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"353fe3ea-6e08-45b2-a051-e8001d0d2d2b","uuid":"f915cf90-ff2e-4b93-b134-514e49581864","timestamp":"2026-05-20T10:07:07.833Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) in a blind academic peer review. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. \n\nReview the following paper and produce a structured review. Use the standard top-ML-venue 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** IRT-FIT: Detecting Benchmark Contamination in Large Language Models via Person-Fit Statistics at the Minimal-Information Operating Point\n\n**Core idea:** Repurpose IRT person-fit statistics (lz*) to detect benchmark contamination in LLMs using only binary correct/incorrect response patterns — the \"minimal-information operating point.\" The key innovation is using Δlz* (difference between clean and contaminated conditions) rather than absolute lz* to handle the universal positive misfit problem in LLMs.\n\n**Three claims:**\n1. On benchmarks with ≥10K items, AUROC > 0.9 at 15% contamination, within 3-5pp of gray-box methods (ConStat, Min-K%++)\n2. Detection power follows a predictable inverted-U curve across contamination levels, with analytically derivable nadir, scaling as a function of benchmark size\n3. Leaderboard-wide audit of 1,847 Open LLM Leaderboard models: ~12% flagged, rising from 6% in 2023H2 to 18% in 2024H1 (p=0.003)\n\n**Method (Section 4):**\n- Pipeline: Fit 4PL IRT model → estimate θ via MLE → compute lz* → flag via |lz*| threshold or Δlz*\n- Calibration gap addressed via relative detection (Δlz* = lz*_test - lz*_clean)\n- Analytical power theory: P(detect) = Φ(−z_α + δ√n / σ_lz*), nadir formula π* ≈ (θ_true − θ_low)/(θ_high − θ_low)\n\n**Experiments (Section 5):**\n- 5.1 Simulation: 5 benchmarks × contamination 5%-100%, 1000 replications. Table 1 shows inverted-U. Power scales with n_items.\n- 5.2 Controlled contamination: Qwen3.5-9B + Gemma-4-31B, QLoRA SFT (rank 16, α=32, lr=2e-4, 3 epochs), 6 levels (0-50%), 5 benchmarks. Tables 3-4. Cohen's d=2.8 (Qwen MMLU 15%), AUROC=0.94. SFT generalization ratio = 4.4%.\n- 5.3 Post-RL robustness: GRPO 2000 steps on 15%-contaminated Qwen. IRT-FIT retains 94% AUROC (0.94→0.88), Min-K%++ drops to 64% (0.96→0.61).\n- 5.4 Baseline comparison: IRT-FIT vs ConStat vs Min-K%++ on ground-truth. Gap <5pp at 15%, 6-10pp at 5%.\n- 5.5 Leaderboard audit: 1,847 models, 6 benchmarks, June 2023-June 2024. 12% overall flagged. Yi-derivatives 31%, Phind-CodeLlama 28%. Temporal trend p=0.003.\n- Appendices: A (lz* decomposition), B (4PL vs 3PL ablation: 3-5pp advantage), C (cross-benchmark comparison), D (minimum n_items table), E (BBH, MATH extended), F (domain-level MMLU analysis), G (multiscale lzm)\n\n**Key tables:**\n- Table 1: Simulation power across contamination levels (5 benchmarks)\n- Table 2: Theory vs simulation vs ctrl-exp power (7 benchmarks, R²≥0.91)\n- Tables 3-4: Dose-response lz* (Qwen, Gemma)\n- Table 5: Post-RL retention (IRT-FIT 94%, Min-K%++ 64%, ConStat 96%)\n- Table 6: Method comparison (AUROC, Type-I, time, archived data applicability)\n- Tables 7-8: Leaderboard audit results and temporal trends\n- Table 9: lz* power decomposition (negative vs positive)\n- Table 10: 4PL vs 3PL ablation\n- Tables 11-14: Extended analyses\n\n**Experiment design reference (from review_context.yaml):** The paper was designed to cover 10+ imagined experiments including extended simulation, controlled contamination pilot + full experiment, analytical power theory, leaderboard recon + audit, 4PL vs 3PL ablation, baseline comparison, multiscale lzm, post-RL test, extra benchmarks (BBH/MATH), and domain-level analysis. All appear reflected in the paper.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate specifically:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the IRT framework correctly applied? Is lz* the right statistic? Is the 4PL model appropriate? Is the Δlz* approach for handling universal misfit well-justified?\n\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Are the experiments well-controlled? Are the simulation assumptions realistic? Is the controlled contamination protocol (QLoRA SFT) a valid proxy for real-world contamination? Is the SFT generalization analysis (4.4% ratio) convincing? Are there confounds?\n\n3. **Statistical validity**: Is the analytical power theory correctly derived? Is the inverted-U well-characterized? Are the AUROC comparisons fair? Is the leaderboard audit's flagging methodology sound?\n\n4. **Reproducibility**: Are sufficient details provided? Could someone reproduce the controlled experiments and the leaderboard audit?\n\n5. **Limitations and scope**: Are the acknowledged limitations honest and complete? What's missing?\n\n6. **Strengths and weaknesses**: List at least 3 of each.\n\n7. **Questions for authors**: List specific questions you would ask.\n\n8. **Overall score** on the 6-point scale with justification.\n\nFormat your review with clear headers: Summary, Strengths, Weaknesses, Questions, Minor Issues, Overall Assessment (score + justification).\n","agentId":"a251e1d4dccb70e3e","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a comprehensive understanding of the paper, the underlying experimental data, the gap between actual results and the paper's claims, and the prior review. Let me now produce my review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: IRT-FIT: Detecting Benchmark Contamination in Large Language Models via Person-Fit Statistics at the Minimal-Information Operating Point\n\n**Reviewer:** R1 (Methods & Experiments focus)\n**Confidence:** 4/5\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper proposes IRT-FIT, a framework that repurposes Item Response Theory person-fit statistics (lz*) to detect benchmark contamination in LLMs using only binary correct/incorrect response patterns. The authors argue this defines a \"minimal-information operating point\" on the contamination detection frontier. The paper introduces a two-sided detection framework (partial contamination produces lz* << 0, full contamination produces lz* >> 0), derives an analytical power formula relating detection power to benchmark size and contamination level, and presents a large-scale audit of 2,847 Open LLM Leaderboard models. Controlled contamination experiments via QLoRA SFT on Qwen3.5-9B and Gemma-4-31B demonstrate AUROC 0.96 at 15% contamination. A post-RL robustness test shows the person-fit signal survives GRPO while MIA-based methods degrade.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n**S1. Well-defined problem positioning.** The \"detection frontier\" concept, ranking methods by information access level (binary < rephrased retest < token probs < weights < training data), is an intellectually clean organizing principle. It clearly articulates IRT-FIT's niche: the only method applicable to historical leaderboard data where only binary outcomes survive.\n\n**S2. Strong power theory with practical value.** The analytical power formula (Eq. 4) fitting simulation data at R^2=0.97, combined with the inverted-U inflection point prediction from item difficulty distributions (error <= 0.02), is the paper's strongest theoretical contribution. Table 8 (minimum n_items for 80% power) translates directly into actionable guidance for practitioners. The honest acknowledgment that ARC-C (1,172 items) yields only 12.9% power — framing it as an information limitation rather than a method failure — is commendable.\n\n**S3. Controlled experiment design.** Using two architecturally distinct base models (Qwen3.5-9B, Gemma-4-31B) across six contamination levels with QLoRA SFT provides clean ground truth. The contamination selectivity analysis (Table 3: +31pp on contaminated items, +1.4-3.7% generalization) is a critical piece of evidence that the SFT signal is sufficiently item-specific for person-fit detection. Cross-architecture consistency (effect sizes within 15%) rules out model-specific artifacts.\n\n**S4. Post-RL robustness finding.** The demonstration that lz* retains 96% AUROC after GRPO while Min-K%++ drops to 70% and reference-based MIA drops to 76% is a differentiated result. The mechanistic explanation — RL recalibrates probabilities but does not undo memorization at the binary outcome level — is well-argued.\n\n**S5. Unprecedented scale for a binary-information method.** The 2,847-model leaderboard audit is something no token-level or re-testing method can replicate on historical data. The temporal trend (6% to 18% flagged rate, 2023-2024) provides community-relevant intelligence.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**W1. Critical discrepancy between simulation and real-data results — the \"universal misfit\" problem is insufficiently addressed. (Major)**\n\nThe paper's memory.md and analysis_report.md reveal that when lz* is applied to real LLM response data from 12 PSN-IRT models, **95% of all model-benchmark cells (57/60) are flagged** — including obviously clean models like GPT-4, DeepSeek-V3, and Mistral-7B across all benchmarks. The actual real-data lz* values are extreme: Mistral-7B on MMLU has lz*=+34.9, Qwen-Plus on MMLU has lz*=+36.2. These are even more extreme than Gemini-1.5's lz*=+31.8, which the paper highlights as the \"smoking gun\" for contamination. Yet the paper presents only the Gemini-1.5 case and selectively reports 4 flagged models from the two-sided framework (Table 18 in review_context.yaml). This selective reporting conceals the fact that absolute lz* thresholding is fundamentally broken for real LLM data.\n\nThe paper introduces the Delta-lz* concept (difference between clean and contaminated conditions) but this is only applicable when ground-truth clean/contaminated pairs exist — i.e., the controlled experiments. For the leaderboard audit (the paper's headline application), there is no clean baseline, and the paper does not clearly explain how the 12% flagging rate was derived given that absolute thresholding flags 95% of models. The methodology for going from \"95% flagged on 12 models\" to \"12% flagged on 2,847 models\" is not transparent.\n\n**W2. The controlled experiments and leaderboard audit appear to use imagined (not actual) results. (Major)**\n\nThe review_context.yaml explicitly labels all experiments as \"imagined_experiments\" with \"expected_results\" and \"result_rationale\" that read as pre-specified predictions rather than observed outcomes. The actual artifacts directory contains only: (1) Phase A simulation results (sim_001), (2) Phase B person-fit analysis on 12 PSN-IRT models, and (3) extended simulation results. There is no evidence of actual QLoRA SFT training runs, no GRPO training logs, no actual leaderboard inference runs on 2,847 models (which would require 18.5 GPU-hours), and no ConStat/Min-K%++ comparison runs. The numbers in the paper tables appear to be the \"expected_results\" from the design document, transcribed as if they were observed.\n\nIf the controlled contamination experiment was not actually run, the AUROC claims, Cohen's d values, selectivity ratios, and post-RL robustness results are all fabricated. This is the single most serious concern.\n\n**W3. lzm null distribution is mathematically incorrect. (Major)**\n\nEquation 3 defines lzm = (1/sqrt(K)) * sum(|lz*_k|). Under the null, lz* ~ N(0,1), so |lz*| follows a half-normal distribution with mean ~0.798 and SD ~0.603. The sum of K half-normal random variables is NOT normally distributed, especially for small K (K=5 benchmarks). The paper claims lzm ~ N(0,1) verified by KS test (p=0.312), which is mathematically impossible for the formula as written. Either the implementation uses signed values (contradicting the formula) or the KS test is against the wrong reference distribution.\n\n**W4. Unfair baseline comparison setup. (Moderate)**\n\nConStat is designed for re-test inconsistency detection, which is orthogonal to the SFT memorization threat model. SFT permanently alters weights, so re-testing yields identical results — this mechanically disadvantages ConStat. Min-K%++ operates on continuous token-level signals, which contain strictly more information than binary responses. Claiming IRT-FIT \"outperforms\" Min-K%++ (0.96 vs 0.88) without noting that Min-K%++ uses a different, richer signal space is misleading. A fairer comparison would hold the information level constant or at least prominently discuss the apples-to-oranges nature.\n\nAdditionally, no confidence intervals are reported on any AUROC values, making it impossible to assess statistical significance of the claimed differences.\n\n**W5. Narrow post-RL validation. (Moderate)**\n\nOnly one RL algorithm (GRPO), one base model (Qwen3.5-9B), one contamination level (15%), and one benchmark (MMLU) are tested. The claim that \"person-fit survives post-RL training\" is stated as a general finding but is tested on a single configuration. PPO, DPO, and KTO may behave differently. The GRPO run duration (2000 steps) is also relatively short — longer RL training or iterated RL might erode the signal further.\n\n**W6. The paper conflates \"flagged\" with \"contaminated\" in the leaderboard audit. (Moderate)**\n\nThe 12% flagged rate is reported alongside temporal trends and model-family breakdowns as if it were a contamination rate. Without external validation (known-contaminated models, correlation with independent contamination evidence), these are statistical anomaly rates, not contamination rates. The alternative explanation — that the flagging reflects IRT model misfit rather than contamination — is supported by the actual data showing 95% flagging on the 12 PSN-IRT models.\n\n**W7. Item parameter dependency is obscured. (Minor)**\n\nThe \"binary responses only\" claim omits that IRT-FIT requires pre-calibrated 4PL item parameters (from PSN-IRT, calibrated on 12 models). These parameters are a non-trivial input that required a separate research effort. The information requirement is more precisely \"binary responses + calibrated item bank,\" which should be explicitly stated.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Actual vs. imagined experiments:** Were the controlled contamination experiments (QLoRA SFT on Qwen3.5-9B and Gemma-4-31B), the post-RL GRPO experiment, and the 2,847-model leaderboard audit actually executed? If so, where are the training logs, model checkpoints, and raw inference outputs? If not, the paper must clearly label all results as \"projected based on simulation.\"\n\n2. **95% flagging rate:** The actual Phase B analysis flags 57/60 model-benchmark cells. How do you reconcile this with the paper's presentation of only 4 flagged models? What methodology was used for the leaderboard audit's 12% flagging rate, given that absolute thresholding flags nearly everything?\n\n3. **lzm formula:** Does the implementation use |lz*| (as in Eq. 3) or signed lz* values? If absolute values, the claimed N(0,1) null is incorrect. Please provide the correct null distribution or correct the formula.\n\n4. **Item parameter calibration:** The PSN-IRT parameters were calibrated on only 12 models. How sensitive is lz* to perturbations in these parameters? What happens with parameters estimated from a different model sample?\n\n5. **Adversarial contamination at the inflection point:** Given the predictable inflection point, could an adversary deliberately contaminate at exactly the inflection fraction (e.g., 65% for MMLU) to evade detection? The inverted-U dip at this point appears to create a systematic blind spot.\n\n6. **ARC-C anomaly:** ARC-C (1,172 items) has much lower power (0.129) than GSM8K (0.578) at similar n_items (1,319). The paper attributes this to item discrimination quality. Can you quantify this effect and incorporate it into the power formula?\n\n---\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n- The abstract states \"2,847\" models but the paper summary says \"1,847\" — inconsistency needs resolution.\n- The abstract claims AUROC 0.96 at 15% contamination; Table 2 shows pooled AUROC 0.96 — these agree but only for MMLU. The claim should specify the benchmark.\n- 11 references is far too few for any top venue. Critical omissions include Karabatsos (2003) on comparing person-fit statistics, Sinharay (2017) on preknowledge detection power, Lalor et al. (2019) and Vania et al. (2021) on IRT for NLP, and Golchin & Surdeanu (2024) on contamination detection.\n- Table count is very high (24 tables). Consolidating related tables would improve readability.\n- The paper title says \"Minimal-Information Operating Point\" but this phrase is not a standard term in the detection literature — it should be more carefully introduced.\n- Wang et al. (2025) citation has a placeholder arXiv ID (2501.xxxxx).\n\n---\n\n### Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 2.5 / 6 (Borderline Findings)**\n\nThe core idea — applying IRT person-fit statistics to detect benchmark contamination using only binary response data — is sound and well-motivated. The \"detection frontier\" framing is clean, the power theory is a genuine contribution, and the leaderboard audit capability is unique. However, two issues prevent me from recommending acceptance:\n\n(1) **Evidence integrity:** The strong suspicion that the controlled contamination experiments, post-RL experiments, and leaderboard audit were not actually executed but rather present \"imagined\" results as empirical findings is disqualifying if true. All paper tables match the \"expected_results\" in the experiment design file verbatim. A paper at a top venue must report actual, reproducible experiments.\n\n(2) **The universal misfit problem is not honestly addressed.** The actual data shows 95% of model-benchmark cells are flagged — meaning absolute lz* thresholding does not work for real LLM data. The paper presents a cleaned-up narrative (only 4 flagged, then 12% on the leaderboard) without transparently explaining how the methodology handles the fundamental violation of IRT assumptions by LLM response patterns. The Delta-lz* approach (which does address this) is only applicable with ground-truth clean baselines, not for the leaderboard audit use case.\n\nIf the controlled experiments are genuine and the universal misfit problem is transparently discussed with a clear methodology for the leaderboard audit, this could be revised to a competitive submission. The power theory and the detection frontier framing have lasting value regardless."}],"totalDurationMs":155301,"totalTokens":93131,"totalToolUseCount":17,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7477,"cache_read_input_tokens":82342,"output_tokens":3311,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7477},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3311,"cache_read_input_tokens":82342,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7477,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7477,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":12,"searchCount":1,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":4}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a0a6b7f6fc67f13d8","tool_use_id":"toolu_01NA4JvGXxwbDbGiReAJA46N","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":94628,"tool_uses":17,"duration_ms":166697},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"d8929f0d-21d0-49cd-9895-2bd1ea7838dd","session_id":"353fe3ea-6e08-45b2-a051-e8001d0d2d2b"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019RRP6vHSFKomN4YALtumbr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KSyoAXTamgmeezV5Y74dMa","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"---\ndream_id: dream_20260520T092614\ntier: best\nreviewer: R2\nfocus: Novelty/Significance and Related Work/Positioning\noverall_score: 3.5/6 Borderline Conference\ntimestamp: 2026-05-20\n---\n\n# R2 Review: IRT-FIT — Novelty, Significance, and Related Work\n\n**Paper:** IRT-FIT: Detecting Benchmark Contamination in Large Language Models via Person-Fit Statistics at the Minimal-Information Operating Point\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nThe paper proposes IRT-FIT, a contamination detection framework that repurposes IRT person-fit statistics (lz*) to flag benchmark contamination in LLMs using only binary correct/incorrect response patterns. The authors frame this as occupying the \"minimal-information operating point\" on a contamination \"detection frontier.\" Three claims are made: (1) AUROC > 0.9 at 15% contamination on large benchmarks, within 3-5pp of gray-box methods; (2) detection power follows a predictable inverted-U with analytical nadir derivation; (3) a leaderboard audit of 1,847 models reveals ~12% flagging with a significant temporal trend. Experiments include simulation, controlled QLoRA SFT contamination on two models, post-RL robustness testing, and baseline comparisons against ConStat and Min-K%++.\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n**S1. The core analogy is genuinely novel and well-exploited.**\nThe connection between \"LLM contaminated on benchmark items\" and \"examinee with item preknowledge\" is clean, correct, and -- to the best of my knowledge -- previously unexplored. This is not merely applying an existing tool to a new domain: the paper identifies that the analogy requires non-trivial adaptation (the calibration gap due to universal positive misfit, the need for Delta-lz* rather than absolute thresholds, the two-sided framework for the inverted-U). The psychometric person-fit literature has studied preknowledge detection for decades (Drasgow et al. 1985, Snijders 2001, Sinharay 2017, Karabatsos 2003), but no prior work has connected this to LLM evaluation. This cross-disciplinary bridge is the paper's strongest intellectual contribution.\n\n**S2. The \"minimal-information operating point\" is a genuine conceptual contribution, not marketing.**\nThe framing is well-defined: binary correct/incorrect is strictly less information than token probabilities, rephrased retesting, or training data access. The paper honestly quantifies the cost of this operating point (3-5pp AUROC gap at 15%, widening to 6-10pp at 5%) and demonstrates the unique capability it enables (retrospective audit of 1,847 archived models that no other method can touch). This is the kind of conceptual framing that organizes thinking for the field -- it answers \"what is the minimal information needed for contamination detection?\" and provides an empirical lower bound.\n\n**S3. The inverted-U characterization is a substantive theoretical finding.**\nThe observation that detection power follows a non-monotonic curve with contamination level -- and that this can be analytically derived from the interaction between contamination fraction and MLE ability estimation -- is non-trivial. The nadir formula (pi* approximately equal to (theta_true - theta_low)/(theta_high - theta_low)) provides a predictive tool. The dual-regime structure (negative lz* from aberrant patterns at low contamination, positive lz* from over-consistency at high contamination) is a genuinely new observation not present in the psychometric cheating detection literature, which typically considers only one-sided tests for preknowledge.\n\n**S4. The leaderboard audit is high-impact and uniquely enabled.**\nAuditing 1,847 models at ecosystem scale, revealing temporal trends (6% to 18%, p=0.003) and identifying high-risk model families (Yi-derivatives 31%, Phind-CodeLlama 28%) is the most practically impactful result. The finding that multi-benchmark flagging has OR=4.2 provides evidence for systematic rather than random misfit. No competing method can perform this audit -- ConStat requires live model retesting, Min-K%++ requires token probabilities unavailable in archives. This unique capability is well-demonstrated.\n\n**S5. Post-RL robustness provides structural differentiation.**\nThe finding that IRT-FIT retains 94% AUROC after GRPO while Min-K%++ drops to 64% is a meaningful result. The explanation is mechanistically clean: RL reshapes token probability distributions but preserves binary response patterns for memorized items. This directly addresses the Wang et al. (2025) critique that MIA methods fail post-RL, and positions IRT-FIT as complementary rather than merely weaker.\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n**W1. [Major] Related work coverage is critically insufficient for a paper claiming cross-disciplinary novelty.**\n\nThe paper cites only 10 references. For a work that claims to bridge psychometrics and NLP, this is unacceptably thin. Specific gaps:\n\n*Person-fit and cheating detection literature (most critical gap):*\n- Sinharay (2017) \"Which Statistic Should Be Used to Detect Item Preknowledge?\" in JEBS -- this is the single most directly relevant psychometric paper. It conducts power analyses for preknowledge detection using lz and other statistics, finding peak detection at 10-30% preknowledge. The inverted-U is implicitly present. Not citing this paper seriously undermines the novelty claim around the power analysis.\n- Karabatsos (2003) comparing 36 person-fit statistics -- why lz* over alternatives like HT, C_aution, U3? Without this discussion, the choice of lz* appears arbitrary.\n- Meijer & Sijtsma (2001) \"Methodology Review: Evaluating Person Fit\" -- the standard survey.\n- van der Flier (1982), Levine & Rubin (1979) -- foundational person-fit references.\n- Wollack & Schoenig (2018) on test security and detection of aberrant behavior in high-stakes testing -- directly analogous application domain.\n\n*Contamination detection (missing recent work):*\n- Ye et al. (2024) \"Data Contamination Quiz\" -- a binary-level detection method. This is a direct competitor operating at a similar information level. Its omission is a serious oversight.\n- Golchin & Surdeanu (2024) \"Time Travel in LLMs\" -- distinct from Oren et al. but related.\n- Balloccu et al. (2024) \"Leak, Cheat, Repeat\" -- systematic study of closed-source LLM contamination.\n- Dekoninck et al. (2024) \"Evading Data Contamination Detection\" -- adversarial perspective.\n- Dong et al. (2024) \"Generalization or Memorization\" -- distinguishing genuine capability from memorization.\n\n*IRT for AI/NLP evaluation:*\n- Lalor et al. (2019) IRT for NLP evaluation.\n- Martinez-Plumed et al. (2019) computational psychometrics for AI.\n- Vania et al. (2021) IRT analysis of NLU benchmarks.\n- Rodriguez et al. (2021) evaluation beyond accuracy with IRT.\n\nThe literature map in the project's own files lists Sinharay (2017) and Karabatsos (2003) as \"foundation\" papers, yet they appear nowhere in the submitted manuscript. This is a puzzling omission.\n\n**W2. [Major] The novelty of the power analysis is overstated given Sinharay (2017).**\n\nSinharay (2017) already conducted power analyses for person-fit statistics under item preknowledge, including the observation that detection power depends on test length and preknowledge proportion. The inverted-U shape -- while the specific two-regime decomposition (negative vs. positive lz*) is new for LLMs -- is partially anticipated by Sinharay's finding that detection power peaks at intermediate preknowledge levels. The paper should clearly delineate what is new relative to Sinharay: (a) the application to LLMs; (b) the two-sided framework motivated by the over-consistency regime; (c) the analytical nadir formula; (d) the benchmark-size thresholds. Without this explicit positioning, the theoretical contribution appears more novel than it is.\n\n**W3. [Major] The \"detection frontier\" framing, while useful, conflates information type with information quantity.**\n\nThe hierarchy \"binary < rephrased retest < token probs < logits < weights\" is presented as a strict ordering of information access. But this conflation is problematic:\n- Rephrased retesting requires *active interaction* (crafting new items, re-evaluating), which is a qualitatively different requirement from passively observing token probabilities.\n- Token probabilities are often freely available via APIs (e.g., OpenAI's logprobs parameter), making them arguably easier to obtain than binary response logs across many items.\n- IRT-FIT requires *pre-calibrated 4PL item parameters* from PSN-IRT, which were calibrated on a specific model population. This is itself a substantial prerequisite that should be listed alongside \"binary responses only.\" The claim of \"minimal information\" is somewhat misleading without acknowledging this dependency.\n\nThe frontier concept would be stronger if it distinguished between information *about the model* (binary responses, token probs, weights) and information *about the test* (pre-calibrated item parameters, rephrased items). IRT-FIT is minimal in model information but non-trivial in test information.\n\n**W4. [Moderate] Comparison fairness issues with baselines.**\n\nThe baseline comparison has two fairness concerns:\n1. *ConStat at 0.97 AUROC at 15%*: ConStat is designed to detect memorization via original-vs-rephrased consistency drops. SFT contamination (item-specific memorization) is ConStat's sweet spot. The comparison is informative but should note that ConStat's advantage comes from leveraging a different signal (consistency) unavailable to IRT-FIT.\n2. *Min-K%++ at 0.96 AUROC at 15%*: The paper claims IRT-FIT is \"within 2pp,\" but at 5% contamination the gap is 10pp (0.78 vs 0.88). The paper's headline number (15% contamination on MMLU) is cherry-picked -- it's the condition where all methods approach ceiling. A fairer comparison would report average AUROC across contamination levels and benchmarks.\n3. *Missing same-information-level baseline*: Ye et al. (2024) \"Data Contamination Quiz\" operates at a similar binary information level. Its omission prevents readers from assessing whether IRT-FIT's improvement over same-level methods justifies the IRT complexity.\n4. *No simple baseline*: An accuracy z-score anomaly detector (flagging models whose accuracy deviates from expected given their overall ability) would test whether the full IRT machinery is necessary or whether simpler statistics suffice.\n\n**W5. [Moderate] The Gemini-1.5 case study is problematic as contamination evidence.**\n\nGemini-1.5-Pro achieving 100% MMLU accuracy with theta=0.78 is presented as strong contamination evidence. But theta=0.78 is estimated from the *same 4PL model* that produces the lz* statistic. If the IRT model is misspecified for a model of genuinely extreme capability (Gemini-1.5 is a frontier model), theta estimation could be severely biased downward, producing a misleadingly low theta and inflated lz*. The paper needs to address: could a genuinely capable model with multidimensional strengths produce this pattern under IRT model misspecification? The unidimensionality assumption is most likely to break down precisely for frontier models with heterogeneous capability profiles.\n\n**W6. [Minor] Only two model families in controlled experiments.**\n\nQwen3.5-9B and Gemma-4-31B are both dense decoder-only transformers of moderate size. The generality claim needs validation on:\n- MoE architectures (e.g., Mixtral)\n- Larger models (70B+) where baseline ability is higher and the contamination signal may be weaker\n- Models with different pretraining paradigms\n- Different contamination injection methods beyond QLoRA SFT\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1.** Why are Sinharay (2017) and Karabatsos (2003) -- listed in your own literature map as \"foundation\" papers -- absent from the manuscript? Sinharay's power analysis for preknowledge detection is the single most directly relevant psychometric precedent.\n\n**Q2.** How does the method perform when item parameters are not pre-calibrated by PSN-IRT? If a practitioner wants to apply IRT-FIT to a new benchmark without PSN-IRT coverage, what is the minimum number of models needed to calibrate item parameters, and how does calibration quality affect detection performance?\n\n**Q3.** Have you considered or tested Ye et al. (2024) \"Data Contamination Quiz\" as a same-information-level baseline? This method also operates on binary response patterns and is a direct competitor.\n\n**Q4.** For the leaderboard audit, how do you distinguish contamination-induced misfit from IRT model misspecification? The 4PL item parameters were calibrated on a specific model population; models from very different architectures or training pipelines may produce systematic misfit that is not contamination-related.\n\n**Q5.** The retention rate in Table post-RL is reported as \"94%\" for IRT-FIT but this appears to be (0.88/0.94)*100 = 93.6%. Meanwhile the lz* signal retention is only 58% (from -8.7 to -5.1). These two numbers tell different stories. Which is the more informative measure of post-RL robustness? Could you report both clearly?\n\n**Q6.** The nadir formula pi* = (theta_true - theta_low)/(theta_high - theta_low) requires knowledge of theta_true, which is the clean model's ability. In the leaderboard audit setting (no clean baseline), how is this estimated? Is the nadir location used in any part of the audit pipeline?\n\n---\n\n## Minor Issues\n\n1. **Reference formatting**: Several bib entries are incomplete (Wu et al. 2026 cuts off mid-entry; Wang et al. 2025 has \"arXiv:2502.xxxxx\" as a placeholder).\n\n2. **Terminology consistency**: The paper alternates between \"person-fit\" and \"misfit\" without clear distinction. In psychometrics, \"misfit\" typically means poor model fit; \"person misfit\" means the person's pattern is inconsistent with the model. Standardize terminology.\n\n3. **Table density**: 11 tables in the main text plus 7 in the appendix is heavy. Consider consolidating the two dose-response tables (Qwen and Gemma) into a single table with a model column.\n\n4. **Limitations section format**: EMNLP 2026 (ARR) requires Limitations as a standalone section after Conclusion, not a \\paragraph within Conclusion. Current formatting risks desk rejection.\n\n5. **\"Minimal-information operating point\" appears 7 times** in the paper. Once defined, subsequent uses could be shortened to \"minimal-information point\" or \"MIOP.\"\n\n6. **HumanEval (164 items)**: Including HumanEval results throughout adds noise -- it consistently fails. Consider dropping it from main tables and noting it once as an expected failure case.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 3.5 / 6 -- Borderline Conference**\n\n**Justification:**\n\n*For conference (4.0):*\nThe paper has a genuinely novel core insight (person-fit for LLM contamination), a useful conceptual contribution (minimal-information operating point / detection frontier), a substantive theoretical finding (inverted-U with analytical derivation), and a unique high-impact application (1,847-model leaderboard audit). The controlled experiments are well-designed with ground-truth labels, and the post-RL robustness finding is a meaningful differentiator. If these were all that mattered, this would be a clear accept.\n\n*Against conference (holding it at 3.5):*\nThree issues pull the score down. First, the critically thin reference list (10 citations) is unacceptable for a paper claiming cross-disciplinary novelty -- particularly the omission of Sinharay (2017), which directly anticipates the power analysis contribution, and Karabatsos (2003), which would contextualize the choice of lz*. Without engaging this literature, the novelty claim is unsubstantiated. Second, the \"detection frontier\" and \"minimal information\" framing partially obscures the method's dependency on pre-calibrated 4PL item parameters, which is a non-trivial prerequisite. Third, the missing same-information-level baseline (Ye et al. 2024) leaves a gap in the comparative evaluation -- readers cannot assess whether IRT's complexity is necessary or whether simpler binary-level methods achieve comparable results.\n\n*Path to 4.0 (Conference):*\n(a) Expand references to 25-30+, properly engaging the person-fit and contamination detection literatures. Explicitly position relative to Sinharay (2017) and Karabatsos (2003).\n(b) Add Ye et al. (2024) as a same-information-level baseline.\n(c) Acknowledge the PSN-IRT item bank dependency explicitly in the information requirements framing.\n(d) Add a simple accuracy-based anomaly detection baseline to justify IRT complexity.\n(e) Fix the Limitations section format for EMNLP compliance.\n\n*Path to 4.5+ (Borderline Award):*\nAll of the above, plus: (f) broaden controlled experiments to 3+ model families including MoE and 70B+; (g) address the systematic positive bias / calibration gap more rigorously with empirical null distribution analysis; (h) add confidence intervals throughout.\n","project":"irt_personfit_contam","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260520T092614","overall_score":"3.5/6 Borderline Conference","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":768,"cache_read_input_tokens":93548,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":768,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01NA4JvGXxwbDbGiReAJA46N","session_id":"353fe3ea-6e08-45b2-a051-e8001d0d2d2b","uuid":"538d52ac-65b1-43ea-a10e-b2eff407b97c"}
